CPU entropy_mul bisect stage A2 — W22-1 chunk-2 follow-on
==========================================================

Date: 2026-05-18
Host: $(uname -n)
Repo: jxl-encoder (sibling workspace --cpu-entropy-mul-bisect)
Parent commit: 6abfb9c9 (W22-1: content-aware entropy_mul gate, opt-in, default OFF)
Build features: __expert butteraugli-loop
Effort: 7

Harness: jxl-encoder/examples/cpu_entropy_mul_bisect_stage_a2.rs

Hypothesis tested
-----------------
Stage A's bfly excursions (max 18.45 %) might be caused by the W22-1
lifts on AFV (0.818→0.95, +16 %) and DCT4X8 (0.859→0.98, +14 %)
rather than IDENTITY/DCT2X2. Stage A2 holds AFV + DCT4X8 at libjxl
reference and re-sweeps the same IDENTITY × DCT2X2 grid.

Sweep design
------------
Same as stage A, but:
  AFV    = 0.817_794_9    (libjxl reference, NOT W22-1 lift)
  DCT4X8 = 0.859_316_37   (libjxl reference, NOT W22-1 lift)
Stage B (photo validation) is NOT repeated in this run (Stage A already
established photos are NOT bit-identical when entropy_mul is overridden).

Result — REFUTES the hypothesis
-------------------------------
Reverting AFV + DCT4X8 to libjxl reference made bfly excursions LARGER
on a different image (windows95 d=0.5 jumped from out-of-grid in Stage A
to +33.21 % in Stage A2), confirming that the lifts on IDENTITY/DCT2X2
have their own destabilization mode independent of AFV/DCT4X8.

NO tuple passes the strict gate. NO tuple passes a relaxed gate
(median Δbytes ≤ 0.5 %, median |Δbfly| ≤ 2 %, n_violate_bfly ≤ 3).

Lowest median Δbytes: IDENTITY=1.20, DCT2X2=1.045 → -0.048 % median
  but +33.21 % max bfly on windows95 d=0.5.

Per-image violation pattern (top-5 by best median Δbytes):
  windows95 d=0.5: +33 % bfly across the ENTIRE grid
    (image has 14-color colormap; lifting IDENTITY makes strategy
    search pick DCT8 for blocks that should stay IDENTITY)
  codec_wiki d=2.0: +14-15 % bfly at any DCT2X2 ≥ 1.045
  terminal d=0.5/d=1.0: ±4-5 % bfly depending on tuple

Interpretation
--------------
The `median(mask1x1) > 95` discriminator (W22-1) groups images that have
fundamentally different responses to IDENTITY lifting. Lifting helps
codec_wiki / terminal at some distances but catastrophically hurts
windows95 / codec_wiki at others. The CPU encoder's AC strategy search
surface around the IDENTITY/DCT8/DCT4X4 boundary is more sensitive than
the GPU encoder's was, because the CPU path runs the FULL
`AdjustQuantBlockAC` after strategy selection (the GPU's slow-path
round-trip) — which amplifies strategy-pick differences into per-block
quantizer differences.

The discriminator needs to be MUCH finer than `median(mask1x1) > 95`
— ideally per-block (or per-tile, like the GPU encoder's per-tile
gaborish gate), or it needs to dispatch on (image content × distance ×
effort) not just content alone.

Honest-stop conclusion
----------------------
W22-1 chunk-2 (default-on flip) CANNOT ship with a global per-image
discriminator. Investigation determined the chunk-3 plan needs to be
re-scoped — `kAvoidEntropyOfTransforms` is already wired (see
`vardct/ac_strategy_search.rs:60-68 avoid_entropy_of_transforms_mul`),
BUT it is gated to `distance > 4.0` and returns 0.0 at every distance
in this bisect's test grid (d ∈ {0.5, 1.0, 2.0}). So the penalty is
NOT active at the distances where the bfly excursions occur — the
strategy search is operating without that penalty at all.

The destabilization happens because at low distances the cost model
relies almost entirely on `(entropy + pixel-domain loss) * entropy_mul`
to discriminate IDENTITY vs DCT8 / DCT4X4. On windows95 (14-color
colormap) the per-block loss is genuinely close between IDENTITY and
DCT4X4 (both reproduce a constant-color block well), so a small lift
on IDENTITY's `entropy_mul` flips many blocks to DCT4X4, which then
gets quantized differently and produces +33 % bfly even though byte
counts barely change.

Three forward paths for chunk-3 (ranked by expected payoff):

(1) Lift `kAvoidEntropyOfTransforms` gate from `d > 4.0` to `d > 0.0`
    (with appropriate distance scaling). This would penalize every
    non-DCT8 strategy at every distance, stabilizing the IDENTITY↔
    DCT8/DCT4X4 boundary. Risk: changes the byte profile at all
    distances; needs a separate chunk to bisect the right `(base,
    formula)` for low-d.

(2) Add a per-block content discriminator (e.g., per-block variance
    or block-local mask1x1 percentile) inside the AC strategy search
    itself, so the lift only applies to blocks with `local_mask >
    threshold` rather than to the whole image. This is closer to
    libjxl's per-tile gaborish dispatch. Higher implementation cost;
    needs the strategy search to accept a per-block multiplier on
    `entropy_mul_table`.

(3) Decompose `screenshot_suppressed()` into per-strategy gates
    rather than a unified table swap. Specifically:
    - DCT4X4 lift is the dominant destabilizer (the strategy that
      windows95 was switching INTO when IDENTITY got lifted).
    - Add `EntropyMulTable::screenshot_suppressed_dct4x4_only()`
      that lifts ONLY DCT4X4 (e.g. 1.08 → 1.30), leaving IDENTITY,
      DCT2X2, AFV, DCT4X8 at reference. Re-bisect.

Chunk-2 stays opt-in (W22-1 current state). No default-on flip
this chunk. CHANGELOG entry documents the negative result so future
agents don't re-attempt the same bisect.

Status: HONEST-STOP. Default `LossyConfig::content_aware_entropy_mul`
remains `false`. Chunk-3 deferred pending chunk-3 implementation
choice (above).

Reproducer
----------
cargo run --release -p jxl-encoder --features '__expert butteraugli-loop' \
    --example cpu_entropy_mul_bisect_stage_a2 -- \
    benchmarks/cpu_entropy_mul_bisect_stage_a2_2026-05-18.tsv
